      "detail": "Two independent open cases, each measured and each green in isolation. Two consecutive runs failed exactly one of them, a different one each time. Nothing is filtered, no assertion is relaxed and no timeout is extended.",
      "openCases": [
        {
          "test": "Receiving_and_grouping_messages_by_ref_type.Should_receive_one_batch_per_group",
          "diagnosis": "Not vague flakiness. The test waits for six consumed messages and then reads _batches, a fixture level list that is filled by an unawaited ContinueWith on each batch task. The failure data proves it: observed < 3, 1 >, missing < 2 >. All three batches were consumed; one append had simply not run yet when the assertion executed.",
          "isolation": "5 of 5 green at a very stable 0.625 s.",
          "fixWouldBe": "Let the test await the batches it expects instead of asserting against a list populated by fire and forget continuations. That changes how the spec synchronises, so it is submitted rather than taken."
        },
        {
          "test": "ContainerTests.KillSwitch_Specs.Should_be_degraded_after_too_many_exceptions",
          "diagnosis": "Genuinely load dependent. WaitForHealthStatus carries its own fresh 15 s source per call, so the shared budget does not explain it; under full load one transition does not complete inside that window.",
          "isolation": "5 of 5 green at a very stable 10.39 s.",
          "improvementMade": "Sampling moved from one second to 50 ms. The budget is unchanged and a status that never arrives still expires, so this can hide nothing. It moved the failure from 20.0 s to 15.1 s and let one full run pass, without removing the sensitivity.",
          "fixWouldBe": "An event driven wait instead of polling. Submitted rather than taken."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
